diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index fe9fb19..f7ebc15 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -47,6 +47,8 @@ Optional file `~/.pi/agent/infinite-context/settings.json` (inside Pi's configur
 
 Each field resolves on its own: the exact `provider/modelId` entry, then the provider entry, then `default`, then `nudgeAtTokens` (start only), then the built-in 75% / 5% of the model window. A start above `nudgeAtPercent` of the window is lowered to it. The `reminders` block is strict: an invalid one, or an unknown key anywhere in the file, rejects the whole file with a warning at session start, and the defaults apply.
 
+A reminder can carry a cost hint: with `prices` (cache write `w` and read `r`, per exact `provider/modelId` or provider; any unit), a warm prompt cache gets the number of turns after which a fold pays off, and a cold one (older than `cacheTtlMinutes`, default 5) "folding is free now". `foldHint` is `"cache-aware"` (default; also adds a short fold-timing paragraph to the system prompt) or `"fixed"` (no hint, no paragraph). `prices`, `cacheTtlMinutes` and `foldHint` are strict like `reminders`.
+
 `nudgeAtTokens` can also be a range, `[100000, 300000]`, to measure how the limit affects cost: each session then draws one limit uniformly from the range and keeps it when resumed, reloaded or forked. A changed range draws anew.
 
 The session records the effective settings as a `custom` entry of type `infinite-context:settings` whenever they change (with `nudgeAtTokensRange` when the limit was drawn), and each reminder carries its token count, window, threshold and step in `details`.
diff --git a/extensions/infinite-context/fold-hint.test.ts b/extensions/infinite-context/fold-hint.test.ts
